Why MPL-2.0 rather than a permissive license: the client is the part a fork
could most easily turn into a competing closed product. File-level copyleft
keeps modifications to existing files open, while still allowing new files,
commercial distribution and app-store publication.

Core libraries consumed by this client (narrative graph, spoiler firewall,
graph renderer, contracts) are separate Apache-2.0 repositories, so another
project can reuse them without inheriting the client's MPL terms.

